<div class="sop-meta">
  <div><strong>Trigger</strong> Editorial calendar; on a fixed cadence regardless of mood</div>
  <div><strong>Owner</strong> Jon</div>
  <div><strong>Time</strong> 5-7 hours per post: research 1h, draft 3-4h, edit 1h, repurpose 1h</div>
  <div><strong>Output</strong> Published post + LinkedIn post + email send + one short-form clip</div>
</div>

<h2>Editorial cadence</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>One post every 10-14 days.</strong> Bi-weekly is the floor; faster is fine in flush months.</li>
  <li><strong>One vertical post per quarter.</strong> Each trade I serve gets at least one industry guide per year. Rotate: HVAC Q1, roofing Q2, plumbing Q3, electrical Q4 (then loop). Inspectors and landscaping fit on top.</li>
  <li><strong>One comparison or platform post per quarter.</strong> Wix / Squarespace / WordPress / AI builders rotation. These are the highest-converting posts; keep them fresh.</li>
  <li><strong>One seasonal post per quarter.</strong> Spring lead-gen, storm season, fall tune-up, winter emergency. Pairs with the relevant <code>/events/</code> landing page.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Topic ideation</h2>
<p>Sources, in priority order:</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Customer questions.</strong> Real questions from discovery calls, /ask/ submissions, and audit replies. The single best topic source, the question is already pre-validated.</li>
  <li><strong>Keyword research.</strong> Google Search Console reports + a free tool (e.g. Keywords Everywhere). Look for queries with 50-500 monthly volume that are NOT yet served by my own content.</li>
  <li><strong>Competitor gaps.</strong> What is the top result for "[trade] website tips"? Read it; identify what is missing. Write the version that fills the gap.</li>
  <li><strong>Glossary as topic engine.</strong> Each glossary term is a candidate for a longer post. "What does PageSpeed actually mean for your business" is a 1,200-word post.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Post structure (target: 1,200-1,800 words)</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Hook.</strong> 2-3 sentences. The problem the post solves, in concrete language.</li>
  <li><strong>Direct answer.</strong> Don't bury it. State the conclusion in the first 100 words.</li>
  <li><strong>Body.</strong> H2-structured, scannable, with bullet lists. 4-7 H2s typical.</li>
  <li><strong>Specifics.</strong> Real numbers, real examples, real names where possible. Generic advice ranks worse and converts worse.</li>
  <li><strong>Internal links.</strong> 3-5 per post, to relevant service / comparison / sample / SOP-public pages.</li>
  <li><strong>One CTA at the bottom.</strong> Tied to a service page or the audit. Not a generic "contact us".</li>
  <li><strong>Schema.</strong> BlogPosting JSON-LD (the layout handles this).</li>
  <li><strong>Tags.</strong> Trade tag (hvac/roofing/etc.) + topic tag (local-seo/comparison/seasonal/case-study).</li>
</ul>

<h2>Repurposing workflow (per post)</h2>
<ul class="sop-checklist">
  <li>Day 0: post goes live; share immediately on LinkedIn personal + business pages with a 3-sentence thesis.</li>
  <li>Day 1: send to the email newsletter list (per <code>/sop/email-newsletter/</code>) with a 1-paragraph framing + the link.</li>
  <li>Day 3: pull 1-2 quotes from the post into a single LinkedIn carousel (or single image post). Different angle from the day-0 share.</li>
  <li>Day 7: short-form clip, a 60-90 second video or a single screenshot of the most-shareable observation. Post on whichever short-form channel I am active on (X, Threads, etc.).</li>
  <li>Quarter-end: review which posts converted (audits, calls). Refresh the top performers; retire the ones that did not earn their place.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Editorial calendar template</h2>
<p>Maintain a single sheet (Google Sheet or Notion):</p>
<ul>
  <li>Slug</li>
  <li>Title (working)</li>
  <li>Target keyword</li>
  <li>Vertical / topic tag</li>
  <li>Funnel stage (top / middle / bottom)</li>
  <li>Internal-link targets (which pages this links to)</li>
  <li>Publish date (planned)</li>
  <li>Status (idea / drafting / review / scheduled / live)</li>
  <li>Refresh date (set 12 months out)</li>
</ul>

<h2>Common issues</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Cadence slips.</strong> Set the calendar 90 days ahead, with topics filled in. Drafting on schedule is easier than ideating on schedule.</li>
  <li><strong>Posts don't convert.</strong> Funnel stage mismatch. Top-of-funnel posts should not have hard CTAs; bottom-of-funnel posts should not have soft CTAs.</li>
  <li><strong>Repurposing is skipped.</strong> The post is half the work; repurposing is the other half. Block it on the calendar at the same time as drafting.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Exit criteria for each post</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Live on the site, indexed, in the right tag taxonomy.</li>
  <li>Sent to the email list.</li>
  <li>3+ repurposing touches across other channels.</li>
  <li>Refresh date set on the calendar 12 months out.</li>
</ul>
